WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
Who judges: the benevolent dictator.

2
00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:06.360
Error analysis needs a decision-maker for “is this a failure?

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:19.099
” The FAQ’s recommendation for most small and medium teams is a single domain expert who acts as the benevolent dictator of quality: they set the standard, and their labels are the ground truth.

4
00:00:19.379 --> 00:00:22.621
The episode covers this at about 25:16.

5
00:00:22.901 --> 00:00:33.931
The FAQ’s reasoning: one expert avoids endless annotation conflicts, and if you feel you need five experts to judge one interaction, your product’s scope may be too broad.

6
00:00:34.211 --> 00:00:45.560
That expert should be whoever best knows what a correct answer looks like for users: often a product manager, a support lead, or a subject-matter specialist, not necessarily an engineer.

7
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:56.635
The FAQ describes engineers and PMs catching different problems (technical versus product failures) and favors a domain expert or PM leading over time.

8
00:00:56.915 --> 00:01:07.325
It also calls outsourcing core error analysis to a labeling vendor usually a big mistake, with exceptions for mechanical labels that don’t need product context.

9
00:01:07.605 --> 00:01:23.157
When you do have several annotators, measure their agreement before trusting combined labels (the FAQ mentions Cohen’s kappa, a chance-corrected agreement statistic), hold alignment sessions on disagreements, and let the expert make the final call.

10
00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:28.323
Disagreements are useful: they usually point at a fuzzy definition in your taxonomy.

11
00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:33.083
For model selection, error analysis is also your best comparison tool.

12
00:01:33.363 --> 00:01:40.381
When you trial a second model or provider, run the same traces through it and code its failures with the same taxonomy.

13
00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:45.952
Two models with the same pass rate can fail in completely different ways, and the ways matter.
